{"id": "16", "question": "wat is dna", "gt": "Based on the given passages, DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a nucleic acid that contains the genetic instructions used in the development and functioning of all known living organisms and some viruses. It is a double helix formed by base pairs attached to a sugar-phosphate backbone, with the base pairs forming the ladder's rungs and the sugar and phosphate molecules forming the vertical sidepieces of the ladder. The order of the nitrogen bases in a DNA sequence forms genes, which provide the instructions for making proteins.", "pred": "DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a nucleic acid that contains the genetic instructions used in the development and functioning of all known living organisms and some viruses. It is made up of nucleotides, each consisting of a phosphate group, a sugar group, and a nitrogen base (adenine, thymine, guanine, or cytosine). The sequence of these bases forms genes that direct cells to make proteins. DNA has a double‑helix structure resembling a ladder, with base pairs as the rungs and a sugar‑phosphate backbone as the sides, and it serves as the hereditary material that can replicate itself.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a nucleic acid that contains the genetic instructions used in the development and functioning of all known living organisms and some viruses. It is made up of nucleotides, each consisting of a phosphate group, a sugar group, and a nitrogen base (adenine, thymine, guanine, or cytosine). The sequence of these bases forms genes that direct cells to make proteins. DNA has a double‑helix structure resembling a ladder, with base pairs as the rungs and a sugar‑phosphate backbone as the sides, and it serves as the hereditary material that can replicate itself.", "time_sec": 9.997, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinki