{"id": "82", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "Symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. Causes of irritable bowel syndrome are unknown but it is believed to be due to a number of factors including alteration in gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.", "pred": "The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhea. The exact cause of IBS is unknown, and it is believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. People with IBS have symptoms that can include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is currently unknown, but it is thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. IBS-D is irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhea. The exact cause of IBS is unknown, and it is believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in GI tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors for IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast, slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. People with IBS have symptoms that can include violent episodes of diarrhea, constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps in the lower half of the belly that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating.", "time_sec": 15.971, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 1531, 6706, 1307, 31720, 1638, 55404, 18134, 1319, 1073, 7814, 1041, 1395, 10548, 14183, 1044, 1809, 1494, 1395, 4914, 1317, 2282, 1562, 1261, 9943, 1307, 19577, 46275, 1319, 74536, 1041, 20000, 20775, 1044, 5376, 25257, 1307, 74456, 7389, 1044, 1321, 1261, 47311, 1294, 1278, 9150, 2396, 1278, 9403, 1321, 1278, 61516, 20000, 1046, 1362, 7814, 6928, 1395, 31720, 1638, 55404, 18134, 1454, 75017, 1046, 1531, 7092, 6706, 1307, 1362, 7814, 1395, 14183, 1044, 1321, 1494, 1395, 14521, 1317, 1402, 3964, 1317, 1261, 2782, 1307, 6763, 1044, 3907, 57627, 1294, 61516, 20000, 76067, 1044, 19577, 25199, 2663, 12305, 1044, 5376, 15404, 1317, 6890, 1044, 1321, 6939, 111503, 4446, 1046, 46745, 6763, 1394, 1362, 7814, 4626, 19577, 20775, 1307, 1278, 8112, 1321, 3709, 46527, 2783, 1319, 70672, 7720, 1044, 8455, 1044, 1505, 4