{"id": "199", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused primarily by bacteria, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci, also known as Streptococcus pyogenes. This is the same bacteria that causes strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria types, such as staphylococcus, are also associated with the disease. The bacteria infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Prior trauma, such as a cut or scrape, insect bite, burn, or needle puncture wound can serve as a source of infection as well as surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures. In rare cases, fungi can lead to this condition.", "pred": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically occur when bacteria enter the body through minor traumas like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle punctures, or through surgical incision sites.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically occur when bacteria enter the body through minor traumas like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle punctures, or through surgical incision sites.", "time_sec": 7.795, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 64788, 