{"id": "203", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the main cause of necrotizing fasciitis is bacterial infection, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus. Additionally, other types of bacteria have also been associated with the disease. Trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds, can also play a role in the development of necrotizing fasciitis. Surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures can also serve as a source of infection. Therefore, the answer is: Bacterial infection, including group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus, and trauma.", "pred": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically occur when bacteria enter the body through minor traumas like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle punctures, or through surgical incision sites.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, most commonly group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), which also cause strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease. Infections typically occur when bacteria enter the body through minor traumas like cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle punctures, or through surgical incision sites.", "time_sec": 7.673, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": 