{"id": "204", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Chard, also known as Swiss chard, is a vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It has a flavor that is stronger and more assertive than spinach, and some people find it bitter. Chard can be eaten raw in salads when it's young, but mature leaves and stalks are usually cooked or sautéed. Chard is actually a type of beet that is grown for its leaves rather than its root. It can produce white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. Chard is highly perishable and should be kept in the refrigerator for only 2 to 3 days.", "pred": "Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima). It is also noted for being extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima). It is also noted for being extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "time_sec": 10.284, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", 