{"id": "205", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Chard is a vegetable that has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked or sautéed. Chard is in the same subspecies as beetroot and is cultivated for leaf production. It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stems are slightly tougher. Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a minimum amount of time.", "pred": "Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima). It is also noted for being extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima). It is also noted for being extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "time_sec": 10.338, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trus