{"id": "206", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the given passages, Swiss chard (also known as just plain chard) is a type of beet plant that has been selected for its leaf production rather than storage root formation. It produces large, tender deep green leaves with thick, crisp stalks. The leaves have a more assertive and sometimes bitter taste compared to spinach. Swiss chard is a perishable vegetable and should be stored in the refrigerator. It can be used raw in salads or cooked like in pizzoccheri.", "pred": "Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima). It is also noted for being extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima). It is also noted for being extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "time_sec": 10.339, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": fal