{"id": "209", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the given passages, chard is a type of vegetable that belongs to the same subspecies as beetroot and is characterized by its large, fleshy leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It has a stronger, more assertive flavor than spinach and can be used raw in salads or cooked like in pizzoccheri. Chard is a beet that has been selected for leaf production rather than storage root formation and can be harvested when young and tender or after maturity. It is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden, but it is extremely perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a maximum of 2-3 days.", "pred": "Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima). It is also noted for being extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation. It is cultivated for its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks, which can be used raw in salads when young or cooked when mature. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor, and it is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, being a descendant of the sea beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima). It is also noted for being extremely perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "time_sec": 10