{"id": "242", "question": "how do crab traps work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how crab traps work:\n\nCrab traps are made of a metal cage with a system of nets inside, which are designed to allow crabs to enter but not escape. The traps have two leads that angle into the pot from the sides and bottom, leading to a narrow slit entrance. The crabs crawl up the leads to reach the bait inside the pot, but cannot exit through the slit. The pot is designed to keep the crabs inside until they can be retrieved by the fisherman.\n\nAdditionally, according to passage 3, crab pots can be equipped with mechanisms that disable them over time, preventing them from continuing to catch crabs after they have been abandoned or become lost. These mechanisms can include biodegradable mesh or dissolvable cords.", "pred": "Crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh reinforced with steel bars. The trap is designed as a large square, about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide, with two ends featuring lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance, and crabs must crawl up the leads to reach their food inside. The design allows crabs to enter but not exit.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh reinforced with steel bars. The trap is designed as a large square, about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide, with two ends featuring lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance, and crabs must crawl up the leads to reach their food inside. The design allows crabs to enter but not exit.", "time_sec": 8.278, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thin