{"id": "244", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "gt": "The FEHA (Fair Employment and Housing Act) and the ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) have several differences. The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Another important difference is the requirement of disability. The ADA requires a physical or mental impairment to substantially limit a major life activity, while the FEHA only requires that a mental and physical disability limit a major life activity, without the need for it to be a substantial limit.", "pred": "One key difference is that the ADA applies to private sector employers with four to fifteen employees and distinguishes between private and public employers, while the FEHA applies to more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability requires a substantial limitation of a major life activity, whereas the FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, without the \"substantial\" requirement.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: One key difference is that the ADA applies to private sector employers with four to fifteen employees and distinguishes between private and public employers, while the FEHA applies to more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability requires a substantial limitation of a major life activity, whereas the FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, without the \"substantial\" requirement.", "time_sec": 7.032, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1},