{"id": "283", "question": "difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, while an adjective clause modifies a noun or pronoun.", "pred": "An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, and it is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, because, if, since, when, where, and while. An adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, and it is introduced by relative pronouns (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or subordinate conjunctions (when and where). For example, in the sentence \"The book that I read was interesting,\" the adjective clause \"that I read\" modifies the noun \"book.\" In contrast, in the sentence \"I read the book because I wanted to,\" the adverb clause \"because I wanted to\" modifies the verb \"read.\"", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, and it is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, because, if, since, when, where, and while. An adjective clause is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or pronoun, and it is introduced by relative pronouns (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or subordinate conjunctions (when and where). For example, in the sentence \"The book that I read was interesting,\" the adjective clause \"that I read\" modifies the noun \"book.\" In contrast, in the sentence \"I read the book because I wanted to,\" the adverb clause \"because I wanted to\" modifies the verb \"read.\"", "time_sec": 11.591, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "toke