{"id": "356", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "gt": "Based on the given passages, company goodwill refers to the positive feelings and loyalty of customers towards a business, gained through providing good services or products, building trust, and creating a strong reputation. Goodwill is important because it can increase customer retention, attract new customers, and make the business more attractive to investors and potential buyers.\n\nAccording to passage 3, goodwill is an intangible asset that represents the value of a business's reputation and customer connections, which can make the connection between the business and its customers permanent.\n\nTherefore, goodwill is important for businesses because it can lead to increased customer loyalty, positive word-of-mouth advertising, and higher sales, ultimately contributing to the long-term success and growth of the business.", "pred": "Company goodwill is the advantage of a business's reputation and its connection with customers, which includes the value of customer attraction to the business's name and reputation. It is important because it increases the customer base, retains old clients, attracts investors, and attracts future buyers. Goodwill is created when a business provides good service or products, leading to customer trust, repeat business, and positive word-of-mouth, which in turn creates a business asset known as goodwill.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Company goodwill is the advantage of a business's reputation and its connection with customers, which includes the value of customer attraction to the business's name and reputation. It is important because it increases the customer base, retains old clients, attracts investors, and attracts future buyers. Goodwill is created when a business provides good service or products, leading to customer trust, repeat business, and positive word-of-mouth, which in turn creates a business asset known as goodwill.", "time_sec": 7.566, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 10889, 3683, 27142, 1395, 1278, 12772, 1307, 1261, 6170, 1681, 30200, 1321, 2246, 7936, 1454, 17365, 1044, 1799, 6987, 1278, 2632, 1307, 14146, 41570, 1317, 1278, 6170, 1681, 2564, 1321, 30200, 1046, 2157, 1395, 4023, 3147, 1494, 11462, 1278, 14146, 4469, 1044, 77311, 3992, 17412, 1044, 127809, 41914, 1044, 1321, 127809, 7147, 69519, 1046, 13649, 27142, 1395, 6254, 2200, 1261, 6170, 8222, 3683, 4810, 1505, 8066, 1044, 8924, 1317, 14146, 12319, 1044, 21788, 6170, 1044, 1321, 6818, 3541, 13144, 3049, 4519, 1044, 1799, 1294, 3884, 21622, 1261, 6170, 24596, 4629, 1435, 3683, 27142, 1046, 11], "logp_list": [0.0, -0.285, -0.0493, -0.2148, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.2676, -0.042, -0.8733, -0.4223, -0.0995, -0.0028, -0.0774, -0.0001, -0.0181, -0.9637, -0.1279, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0065, -0.9562, -1.2936, -0.2467, -0.9884, -0.0145, -0.1462, -0.0519, -1.1806, -0.0286, -0.6625, -0.3657, -0.0048, -0.0007, -0.0288, -0.4606, -0.1174, -0.0085, -0.0049, -0.0988, -0.0271, -0.0907, -0.2724, -0.0016, -0.0, -0.0448, -0.0299, -0.0117, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0247, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0038, -0.0077, -0.0032, -0.0, -0.0913, -0.8296, -0.0008, -0.4901, -0.2586, -0.006, -0.091, -0.0, -0.0033, -0.0026, -0.0088, -0.0001, -0.0224, -0.0013, -0.0007, -0.0422, -0.0259, -0.0, -0.0673, -0.0004, -0.166, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0352, -0.0003, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4005, -0.0338, -0.1986, -0.0001, -0.437, -0.005, -0.5185, -0.0, -0.6691, -0.0, -0.0021, -0.0003, -0.045, -0.215], "cumulative_logprob": -15.2992, "norm_logp": -0.1561, "input_len": 367, "output_tokens": 98, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 41.27, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "357", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "An ad hominem fallacy occurs when someone attacks the character, motive, or other personal attribute of the person making an argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. This is often done to invalidate their arguments by associating their character with the soundness of their argument. On the other hand, the poisoning the well fallacy, which is a type of ad hominem attack, occurs when a person's integrity or intelligence is attacked before the merits of a case can be considered, effectively redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than addressing the argument directly. In contrast, the poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties and attacking the person's integrity or intelligence.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than addressing the argument directly. In contrast, the poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties and attacking the person's integrity or intelligence.", "time_sec": 6.609, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 1531, 1683, 4610, 125350, 6962, 5245, 19263, 4815