{"id": "518", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, who have the opportunity to win prizes. The specific details and mechanics of a raffle can vary. In a reverse raffle, participants purchase tickets with numbers on them, and the last ticket drawn wins the main prize. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the proceeds go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is allocated for a charitable purpose. In some cases, raffles may also offer additional opportunities to win prizes, such as cash tip board raffles.", "pred": "Raffles work by selling tickets, where each ticket has a number (e.g., 1 to 55 in a reverse raffle), and a portion of the ticket sales (gross proceeds) is allocated to prizes. In a reverse raffle, a table of 20 individuals (one complete ticket at $1000) participates in drawings for $500 prizes (early bird and first ticket pulled). In a 50-50 draw, 50% of ticket sales go to the winner of a random draw, and the remaining 50% is set aside for charity. Tickets may include additional benefits like drinks and dinner, and separate cash tip board raffles offer chances to win $10,000.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Raffles work by selling tickets, where each ticket has a number (e.g., 1 to 55 in a reverse raffle), and a portion of the ticket sales (gross proceeds) is allocated to prizes. In a reverse raffle, a table of 20 individuals (one complete ticket at $1000) participates in drawings for $500 prizes (early bird and first ticket pulled). In a 50-50 draw, 50% of ticket sales go to the winner of a random draw, and the remaining 50% is set aside for charity. Tickets may include additional benefits like drinks and dinner, and separate cash tip board raffles offer chances to win $10,000.", "time_sec": 11.888, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "en