{"id": "548", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "A transistor is a miniature electronic component made of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It can function as an amplifier or a switch. As an amplifier, it takes a small electric current at one end (input current) and produces a larger electric current (output current) at the other end. As a switch, it uses a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a larger supply of electricity. The transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base. The amount of collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current.", "pred": "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, allowing it to function as an amplifier by taking in a tiny electric current at one end and producing a much bigger electric current at the other, or as a switch by controlling the flow of current from the emitter to the collector through the base, where the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to a more positive voltage, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, allowing it to function as an amplifier by taking in a tiny electric current at one end and producing a much bigger electric current at the other, or as a switch by controlling the flow of current from the emitter to the collector through the base, where the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to a more positive voltage, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "time_sec": 9.866, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 1349, 31064, 1395, 1261, 25497, 4968, 2434, 1317, 110521, 1321, 7530, 15356, 12305, 1321, 16878, 4053, 1044, 16815, 1307, 25497, 4318, 1454, 1513, 4403, 3300, 41673, 1394, 7936, 1317, 1420, 10801, 9177, 1046, 2157, 6113, 1536, 2505, 1261, 3709, 5647, 1307, 32741, 1317, 3119, 1261, 15085, 1408, 1261, 3315, 9039, 11299, 1307, 32741, 1044, 15249, 1494, 1317, 2254, 1435, 1420, 41344, 1536, 8198, 1294, 1261, 27465, 12093, 3519, 1513, 1925, 2362, 1321, 20044, 1261, 3315, 28251, 12093, 3519, 1513, 1278, 2147, 1044, 1505, 1435, 1261, 7530, 1536, 23005, 1278, 5692, 1307, 3519, 1562, 1278, 78429, 1317, 1278, 46975, 2645, 1278, 4469, 1044, 2478, 1278, 4469, 55888, 35572, 1562, 1278, 78429, 3964, 1317, 1261, 2081, 6818, 11258, 1044, 11851, 1261, 5692, 1307, 32741, 2645, 1278, 31064, 1046, 11], "logp_list": [0.0, -0.148, -0.0343, -0.0152, -0.0004, -0.0007, -0.0012, -0.3812, -0.0405, -0.554, -0.0204, -0.0006, -0.0158, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0069, -0.0054, -0.0, -0.414, -0.0236, -0.0005, -0.0044, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.518, -0.0167, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0431, -0.0373, -0.4304, -0.1585, -0.0756, -0.0114, -0.0002, -0.0125, -0.0, -0.0008, -0.0707, -0.0, -0.0088, -0.2219, -0.006, -0.0, -0.0241, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0541, -1.3538, -0.5782, -0.0, -0.0112, -0.0012, -0.0419, -0.0, -0.8195, -0.0598, -0.0014, -0