{"id": "550", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is an electronic component made of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It functions as an amplifier or a switch depending on the flow of electrons through its base. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current at one end and produces a larger electric current at the other. The flow of electrons is controlled by using a small amount of electricity to turn a valve-like structure in the transistor. In its off state, the holes in the base act as a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector. Transistors can have common-emitter configuration where collector current is approximately β times the base current.", "pred": "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, allowing it to function as an amplifier by taking in a tiny electric current at one end and producing a much bigger electric current at the other, or as a switch by controlling the flow of current from the emitter to the collector through the base, where the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to a more positive voltage, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, allowing it to function as an amplifier by taking in a tiny electric current at one end and producing a much bigger electric current at the other, or as a switch by controlling the flow of current from the emitter to the collector through the base, where the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to a more positive voltage, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "time_sec": 9.83, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 1349, 31064, 1395, 1261, 25497, 4968, 2434, 1317, 110521, 1321, 7530, 15356, 12305, 1321, 16878, 4053, 1044, 16815, 1307, 25497, 4318, 1454, 1513, 4403, 3300, 41673, 1394, 7936, 1317, 1420, 10801, 9177, 1046, 2157, 6113, 1536, 2505, 1261, 3709, 5647, 1307, 32741, 1317, 3119, 1261, 15085, 1408, 1261, 3315, 9039, 11299, 1307, 32741, 1044, 15249, 1494, 1317, 2254, 1435, 1420, 41344, 1536, 8198, 1294, 1261, 27465, 12093, 3519, 1513, 1925, 2362, 1321, 20044, 1261, 3315, 28251, 12093, 3519, 1513, 1278, 2147, 1044, 1505, 1435, 1261, 7530, 1536, 23005, 1278, 5692, 1307, 3519, 1562, 1278, 78429, 1317, 1278, 46975, 2645, 1278, 4469, 1044, 2478, 1278, 4469, 55888, 35572, 1562, 1278, 78429, 3964, 1317, 1261, 2081, 6818, 11258, 1044, 11851, 1261, 5692, 1307, 32741, 2645, 1278, 31064, 1046, 11], "logp_list": [0.0, -0.148, -0.0343, -0.0152, -0.0004, -0.0007, -0.0012, -0.3812, -0.0405, -0.554, -0.0204, -0.0006, -0.0158, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0069, -0.0054, -0.0, -0.414, -0.0236, -0.0005, -0.0044, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.518, -0.0167, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0431, -0.0373, -0.4304, -0.1585, -0.0756, -0.0114, -0.0002, -0.0125, -0.0, -0.0008, -0.0707, -0.0, -0.0088, -0.2219, -0.006, -0.0, -0.0241, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0541, -1.3538, -0.5782, -0.0, -0.0112, -0.0012, -0.0419, -0.0, -0.8195, -0.0598, -0.0014, -0.0, -0.0031, -0.3205, -0.0011, -0.3787, -0.463, -0.0, -0.6233, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0061, -0.0019, -0.0, -0.0451, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0199, -0.0011, -0.001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0135, -0.371, -0.2755, -0.1399, -0.0, -0.5983, -0.2756, -0.0027, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0512, -0.0165, -0.0016, -0.3744, -0.5776, -0.7693, -0.0893, -0.1198, -0.0057, -0.0016, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1461, -0.0, -0.2677, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0368, -0.0143, -0.008, -0.0089, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.2152, -0.0, -0.0031, -0.0696, -0.0659], "cumulative_logprob": -12.6139, "norm_logp": -0.097, "input_len": 478, "output_tokens": 130, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.95, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "551", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how a transistor works:\n\n1. A transistor functions as both an amplifier and a switch.\n2. The transistor's design allows it to control a larger supply of electricity with a small amount of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water.\n3. The base of the transistor pulls electrons from the emitter, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.\n4. The transistor can take in a tiny electric current at one end (the input current) and produce a much bigger electric current at the other end (the output current) when it works as an amplifier.\n5. The holes in the base of the transistor act as a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector when the transistor is in its off state.\n6. When the electrons and holes move across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, the transistor begins to work.\n7. The collector current is approximately beta (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, and it is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications.\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages.", "pred": "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, allowing it to function as an amplifier by taking in a tiny electric current at one end and producing a much bigger electric current at the other, or as a switch by controlling the flow of current from the emitter to the collector through the base, where the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to a more positive voltage, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, allowing it to function as an amplifier by taking in a tiny electric current at one end and producing a much bigger electric current at the other, or as a switch by controlling the flow of current from the emitter to the collector through the base, where the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to a more positive voltage, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.", "time_sec": 9.946, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 1349, 31064, 1395, 1261, 25497, 4968, 2434, 1317, 110521, 1321, 7530, 15356, 12305, 1321, 16878, 4053, 1044, 16815, 1307, 25497, 4318, 1454, 1513, 4403, 3300, 41673, 1394, 7936, 1317, 1420, 10801, 9177, 1046, 2157, 6113, 1536, 2505, 1261, 3709, 5647, 1307, 32741, 1317, 3119, 1261, 15085, 1408, 1261, 3315, 9039, 11299, 1307, 32741, 1044, 15249, 1494, 1317, 2254, 1435, 1420, 41344, 1536, 8198, 1294, 1261, 27465, 12093, 3519, 1513, 1925, 2362, 1321, 20044, 1261, 3315, 28251, 12093, 3519, 1513, 1278, 2147, 1044, 1505, 1435, 1261, 7530, 1536, 23005, 1278, 5692, 1307, 3519, 1562, 1278, 78429, 1317, 1278, 46975, 2645, 1278, 4469, 1044, 2478, 1278, 4469, 55888, 35572, 1562, 1278, 78429, 3964, 1317, 1261, 2081, 6818, 11258, 1044, 11851, 1261, 5692, 1307, 32741, 2645, 1278, 31064, 1046, 11], "logp_list": [0.0, -0.148, -0.0343, -0.0152, -0.0004, -0.0007, -0.0012, -0.3812, -0.0405, -0.554, -0.0204