{"id": "641", "question": "differences and similarities between red and white blood cells", "gt": "Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are found in the blood and are part of the circulatory system. They are both formed from pluripotent stem cells in the red bone marrow and contain cell membranes and cytoplasm. The main difference is their functions. RBCs are responsible for delivering oxygen from the lungs to the tissue in the body, while WBCs are part of the immune system and help the body combat diseases, viruses, etc.", "pred": "Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to body tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system that combats disease, viruses, and other pathogens. RBCs lack a nucleus and have a biconcave shape with cytoplasm, whereas WBCs have a nucleus and are involved in immune responses.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to body tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system that combats disease, viruses, and other pathogens. RBCs lack a nucleus and have a biconcave shape with cytoplasm, whereas WBCs have a nucleus and are involved in immune responses.", "time_sec": 8.163, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 30207, 2754, 1058, 12953, 4804, 6517, 3459, 1319, 1082, 6535, 1115, 1041, 1321, 7496, 6517, 3459, 131