{"id": "643", "question": "differences and similarities between red and white blood cells", "gt": "Based on the given passages, we can see that the primary difference between red and white blood cells is their respective functions within the body. Red blood cells are responsible for oxygen delivery to tissues, while white blood cells are part of the immune system and help combat diseases and viruses. However, both types of cells share some common features, such as being formed elements of blood, being formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow, having a cell membrane and cytoplasm, and circulating in the fluids in blood vessels, which is pumped by the heart.", "pred": "Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to body tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system that combats disease, viruses, and other pathogens. RBCs lack a nucleus and have a biconcave shape with cytoplasm, whereas WBCs have a nucleus and are involved in immune responses.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: Similarities: Both red blood cells (RBCs) and white blood cells (WBCs) are formed elements of blood and are formed from pluripotent stem cells in red bone marrow. Differences: RBCs are responsible for oxygen delivery from the lungs to body tissues, while WBCs are part of the immune system that combats disease, viruses, and other pathogens. RBCs lack a nucleus and have a biconcave shape with cytoplasm, whereas WBCs have a nucleus and are involved in immune responses.", "time_sec": 8.215, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "tok