{"id": "644", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, first cut large potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, being careful not to break the long sprouts. Place these chunks on a tray and let them dry out in a cool, dark place overnight. Small, sprouted potatoes can be planted whole. Next, prepare your planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches, removing any large rocks or debris. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut your potatoes into cubes of about 1 to 2 inches, making sure to leave a sprout on each cube. Place these cubes in the trench, sprouted side facing up, spaced about 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the potatoes thoroughly and regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Finally, add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it at least 4 inches away from the sprouting potato vines. This mulch will help keep the soil cool and maintain a consistent moisture level.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts remain intact. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Alternatively, small potatoes can be planted whole after being watered thoroughly. When planting, turn the soil to a depth of 12 inches, remove rocks and debris, and dig a 4-inch wide trench 6 to 8 inches deep. Space rows 24 to 36 inches apart if planting multiple rows. Place the potato chunks in the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing each cube 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it 4 inches away from the sprouting vines to maintain soil coolness and moisture.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts remain intact. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Alternatively, small potatoes can be planted whole after being watered thoroughly. When planting, turn the soil to a depth of 12 inches, remove rocks and debris, and dig a 4-inch wide trench 6 to 8 inches deep. Space rows 24 to 36 inches apart if planting multiple rows. Place the potato chunks in the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing each cube 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it 4 inches away from the sprouting vines to maintain soil coolness and moisture.", "time_sec": 13.965, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 3870, 7411, 1261, 75581, 7239, 68334, 1044, 2158, 6945, 1494, 2203, 63308, 1454, 2295, 1505, 3300, 5731, 2744, 1044, 40227, 1278, 2730, 75581, 14694, 10399, 28890, 1046, 22435, 1278, 63308, 1408, 1261, 40680, 1294, 1261, 12921, 1044, 8500, 7285, 32164, 1317, 12689, 1278, 6945, 15202, 1046, 37346, 1044, 3709, 73710, 1710, 1402, 54952, 6836, 2453, 3144, 4180, 1286, 43850, 1046, 4925, 78398, 1044, 3884, 1278, 14784, 1317, 1261, 12883, 1307, 1032, 1049, 1050, 29466, 1044, 8559, 34176, 1321, 50629, 1044, 1321, 5645, 1261, 1032, 1052, 44830, 9924, 79792, 1032, 1054, 1317, 1032, 1056, 29466, 8378, 1046, 20659, 12964, 1032, 1050, 1052, 1317, 1032, 1051, 1054, 29466, 13292, 1693, 78398, 6245, 12964, 1046, 22435, 1278, 68334, 63308, 1294, 1278, 79792, 1454, 1278, 75581, 7239, 1505, 12128, 4258, 22116, 2015, 1044, 37891, 2744, 50061, 1032, 1054, 1317, 1032, 1056, 29466, 13292, 1046, 31652, 1454,