{"id": "647", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to plant a potato that has sprouted:\n\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, making sure to not break the long sprouts.\n2. Allow the cut ends to dry out overnight in a cool, dark location.\n3. Plant the potato chunks or whole potatoes in well-draining soil, spacing them 24 to 36 inches apart.\n4. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, and place the potato pieces in the bottom of the trench with the sprouted side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n\nNote that the passages do not provide specific information on how to care for the potatoes after planting, such as watering and fertilization.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts remain intact. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Alternatively, small potatoes can be planted whole after being watered thoroughly. When planting, turn the soil to a depth of 12 inches, remove rocks and debris, and dig a 4-inch wide trench 6 to 8 inches deep. Space rows 24 to 36 inches apart if planting multiple rows. Place the potato chunks in the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing each cube 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it 4 inches away from the sprouting vines to maintain soil coolness and moisture.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_dola_nemotron", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, first cut it into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts remain intact. Place the chunks on a tray in a cool, dark location overnight to dry the cut ends. Alternatively, small potatoes can be planted whole after being watered thoroughly. When planting, turn the soil to a depth of 12 inches, remove rocks and debris, and dig a 4-inch wide trench 6 to 8 inches deep. Space rows 24 to 36 inches apart if planting multiple rows. Place the potato chunks in the trench with the sprouted or eye side facing up, spacing each cube 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover with a layer of compost, then replace the dirt on top. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it 4 inches away from the sprouting vines to maintain soil coolness and moisture.", "time_sec": 13.906, "nemotron": {"runtime": "transformers-5.6.2-trust-remote-code", "enable_thinking": false, "media": {"video": false, "audio": false, "image": false}, "premature_layer": 28, "alpha": 0.1}, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [1010, 31106, 1058, 3870, 7411, 1261, 75581, 7239, 68334, 1044, 2158, 6945, 1494, 2203, 63308, 1454, 2295, 1505, 3300, 5731, 2744, 1044, 40227, 1278, 2730, 75581, 14694, 10399, 28890, 1046, 22435, 1278, 63308, 1408, 1261, 40680, 1294, 1261, 12921, 1044, 8500, 7285, 32164, 1317, 12689, 1278, 6945, 15202, 1046, 37346, 1044, 3709, 73710, 1710, 1402, 54952, 6836, 2453, 3144, 4180, 1286, 43850, 1046, 4925, 78398, 1044, 3884, 1278, 14784, 1317, 1261, 12883, 1307, 1032, 1049, 1050, 29466, 1044, 8559, 34176, 1321, 50629, 1044, 1321, 5645, 1261, 1032, 1052, 44830, 9924, 79792, 1032, 1054, 1317, 1032, 1056, 29466, 8378, 1046, 20659, 12964, 1032, 1050, 1052, 1317, 1032, 1051, 1054, 29466, 13292, 1693, 78398, 6245, 12964, 1046, 22435, 1278, 68334, 63308, 1294, 1278, 79792, 1454, 1278, 75581, 7239, 1505, 12128, 4258, 22116, 2015, 1044, 37891, 2744, 50061, 1032, 1054, 1317, 1032, 1056, 29466, 13292, 1046, 31652, 1454, 1261, 6240, 1307, 62042, 1044, 2430, 14422, 1278, 47945, 1408, 3977, 1046, 6225, 1261, 1032, 1052, 44830, 6240, 1307, 15410, 1331, 3879, 1278, 12474, 1044, 18528, 1494, 1032, 1052, 29466, 5109, 1562, 1278, 75581, 26717, 1330, 2783, 1317, 7766, 14784, 12921, 365